# Line-ending policy. Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which
# checks every text file out with CRLF. Without this file that silently broke
# the test suite on a Windows checkout -- 7 of 675 tests failed, none of them
# a real defect (2026-08-13, first Windows clone):
#
#   * tests/unit/claude-md-split.test.js searches for "\n## Heading\n"; a CRLF
#     file has "\r\n## Heading\r\n", so every section lookup missed and four
#     index tests reported CLAUDE.md as missing its headings.
#   * The same suite's per-bullet byte budget inflated by exactly one byte per
#     line, pushing grandfathered bullets over a ratchet they had not grown by.
#   * tests/unit/ui-harness-vendor.test.js (F-11) hashes the vendored Chart.js
#     bundle's RAW BYTES against a pinned sha256; a rewritten line ending
#     changes the bytes and the hash can never match.
#
# Those failures are indistinguishable from real ones at a glance, which is
# the actual cost: a developer either chases a phantom or learns to ignore a
# red suite. LF everywhere removes the ambiguity.
#
# The repo was already LF-clean when this landed, so normalization rewrites
# nothing -- it pins the state rather than changing it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# scripts/*.sh MUST be LF regardless: a CRLF shebang line makes the kernel
# look for an interpreter named "bash\r", which fails with a confusing
# "bad interpreter" error. Covered by the rule above; stated explicitly
# because it is the one case where CRLF breaks execution, not just a test.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Vendored bundles are byte-pinned by sha256 in tools/ui-harness/vendor/
# VERSIONS.json (F-11). Mark them binary so git NEVER rewrites their content
# on any platform -- for a hash-pinned artifact, "auto-detected as text and
# normalized" is exactly the behavior that must not happen.
tools/ui-harness/vendor/*.js -text

# Images and fonts, should any be committed later (screenshots for docs, an
# embedded font). Nothing matches today; here so the first one added does not
# get corrupted by normalization.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
